Usage: worktree prune [--repo PATH] [--dryrun] [--json|--format json] [--verbose] [--remove] [--no-sync]

Global, removal-by-default cleanup of registered worktrees, grouped by canonical directory. Without --repo it enumerates every repository identity the directory registry records (even outside Git) and resolves each through its main checkout, skipping an identity it cannot verify with a bounded reason instead of guessing; --repo PATH restricts the pass to one verified checkout. A directory is removed only when it is a verified non-main, clean directory (the narrow root `.cache` exception is preserved); every associated task is confirmed closed through its own durable task or a proven legacy route; a fresh host occupancy check reports no running or unassociated session in it; and every present idle session the directory's registry associations name is safely relocated to the verified main checkout. A confirmed closed historical task does not keep the directory; a still-open associated task, an unavailable or incomplete occupancy listing, an unresolved session, a mismatched path or session id, a running session, or an unconfirmed move keeps it. Absence of the recorded path is reported factually as absent and preserves the registry evidence without a provider read or a move. The read-only classification never moves a session; the preview then reads each candidate's fresh occupancy and the lineage the host reports, so it reports the same blockers and the same move eligibility the removal would meet, without moving, deleting, or writing anything. --dryrun (alias --dry-run) is the read-only preview and creates no store, schema, or operation lock. The removal stage is the only place that may move a session. There is no waiting period or age threshold. The local guards — never the main checkout, never a dirty or untracked directory, except the exact root `.cache` exception — are decided before any running-session query, so a main-checkout or dirty directory makes zero host or provider calls. The running-session answer comes from the one connected service; coverage is that service, not a whole-machine multi-service inventory. The default output follows the global --output auto selection: the compact human summary on an interactive terminal and the machine-readable aggregate envelope on a pipe or in CI, while --json or --format json still forces the envelope, --format text forces the summary, and a legacy selection that contradicts an explicit --output is rejected. The summary is one heading, one aggregate count line, one kept-reason counts line, at most five removal-failure examples with their path and reason plus the omitted count, a bounded absent-description line, a skipped-repository count when nonzero, and the measured elapsed time; its size never grows with the number of directories, because it lists no per-directory row. --verbose keeps that summary and adds one bounded row per actual removal, per removal failure, per eligible preview row (never labelled as removed), per absent directory, and per kept directory with its reason; it changes nothing in the machine document. The aggregate envelope is the complete-detail inspection channel. --remove is an accepted compatibility no-op and --no-sync is accepted but no longer runs a sync pass: prune never syncs. Branches and registry rows are never deleted by a dry run; a removed directory's registry row is deleted only after the tree is gone. Progress is a human affordance: on an interactive terminal one stderr line is redrawn in place through scanning, verification and deletion, showing completed repositories against the known repository total and cumulative completed directory counts for removal, absence, retention and failure, never a directory path or completion before an action returns, and it is cleared before the result. Orchestrator-only.
